--TEST--
`executeMany` answers a bare `uint` where `execute` answers a `Core\Db\Write`, because a sum has no one execution for `changed` or `lastId` to be about — `rule:core-classes/db-one-api` and `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-one-api` removes the `prepare` step and names the batch as the one case
// that would otherwise still want a `Statement` object: `executeMany` is one
// prepare against many bind sets, so a thousand writes cost the round trips one
// statement costs and the caller writes no loop.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// What it answers is the sum — the number the hand-written loop around
// `PDOStatement::execute` would have accumulated, and nothing else.
uint $written = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (id, owner) values (?, ?)", [
    [1, "ada"],
    [2, "grace"],
]);

// And the boundary `execute` sits on the other side of. That member answers a
// `Write` with three readings on it; this one cannot, because `changed` and
// `lastId` would each have to pick one of the executions to be about.
Core\Db\Write $write = $db->executeMany("delete from accounts where id = ?", [[1]]);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Write`, found `uint`
   --> case.nvs:18:24
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
